      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\), we start by simplifying each term in the sequence.\n\nThe general term in the sequence is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). We need to determine the range of values that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can take as \\(n\\) ranges from 1 to 1000.\n\nFirst, we find the smallest value of \\(n\\) such that \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) is an integer. This occurs when \\(n^2 \\approx k \\cdot 1000\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Therefore, the smallest \\(n\\) is the integer part of \\(\\sqrt{1000} \\approx 31.62\\), so the smallest \\(n\\) is 32.\n\nNext, we find the largest value of \\(n\\) such that \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) is an integer. This occurs when \\(n^2 \\approx k \\cdot 1000\\) for some integer \\(k\\). Therefore, the largest \\(n\\) is \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{1000 \\cdot 1000} \\rfloor = \\lfloor 10000 \\rfloor = 1000\\).\n\nNow, we need to check the values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) for \\(n\\) from 32 to 1000. As \\(n\\) increases from 32 to 1000, \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) increases. We need to find the values that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) takes.\n\nThe smallest value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{32^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1024}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1\\), and the largest value is \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 10000 \\right\\rfloor = 1000\\).\n\nWe need to check if \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) takes all integer values from 1 to 1000. As \\(n\\) increases, \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\) increases, and so does \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). For each \\(k\\) from 1 to 1000, there exists at least one \\(n\\) such that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = k\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct values that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\dots, \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can take is the number of integers from 1 to 1000, which is 1000.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{1000}\\).",
