      "text": "To determine the number of distinct numbers in the list \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\nwe first simplify each term in the sequence. Notice that \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) means the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\). For \\(n = 1000\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000000}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 1000 \\right\\rfloor = 1000.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 999\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{999^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{998001}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 998.001 \\right\\rfloor = 998.\n\\]\n\nWe can generalize this pattern. For any \\(n\\), the value \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) is the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(\\frac{n^2}{1000}\\). As \\(n\\) decreases from 1000 to 1 in steps, the values of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) will form a sequence from 1000 down to 1. This is because \\(n^2\\) decreases from 1000000 to 1, and dividing by 1000 results in a sequence that decreases from 1000 to 1.\n\nTo find the number of distinct integers in this sequence, we need to determine how many integers \\(k\\) exist such that \\(1000 \\geq k \\geq 1\\). This sequence is simply the integers from 1000 to 1, inclusive. The number of integers in this range is:\n\n\\[\n1000 - 1 = 999.\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{999}\\).",
