      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor,\n\\]\n\nwe start by simplifying the general term in the sequence. For each \\( n \\), the term is\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine the range of values that this expression can take. Note that \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\) is an integer if and only if \\( n^2 \\) is divisible by 1000. Therefore, \\( n \\) must be such that \\( n^2 \\) is a multiple of 1000. The prime factorization of 1000 is \\( 1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3 \\). For \\( n^2 \\) to be a multiple of \\( 1000 \\), \\( n \\) must be of the form \\( 2^a \\times 5^b \\) where \\( 3 \\leq a \\leq 10 \\) and \\( 3 \\leq b \\leq 10 \\). This means \\( n \\) can be any integer from 50 to 1000 in steps of 25 (since \\( 50 \\leq n \\leq 1000 \\) and \\( n \\) must be of the form \\( 5 \\times 5^k \\) for some integer \\( k \\) where \\( 3 \\leq k \\leq 4 \\) to ensure \\( n \\leq 1000 \\)).\n\nThus, \\( n \\) can take 21 values (50, 55, 60, ..., 975, 1000). For each \\( n \\), the value of \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor \\) will be unique because \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\) will take on distinct values for each distinct \\( n \\).\n\nTo confirm, let's check the range of \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\). The minimum value occurs when \\( n = 50 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50^2}{1000} = \\frac{2500}{1000} = 2.5.\n\\]\n\nThe maximum value occurs when \\( n = 975 \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{975^2}{1000} = \\frac{950625}{1000} = 950.625.\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\) takes on all integer values from 2 to 950. The number of distinct integers in this range is \\( 950 - 2 + 1 = 949 \\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct numbers in the list is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1000}.\n\\]",
